.         * Control Variables
.         *******************
.         
.         * Marital status
.         label define label_famst 1 "married" 2 "single" 3 "separated/divorced
> " 4 "widowed"

.         recode pgfamstd (1 6 7 = 1) (3 = 2) (2 4 8 = 3) (5 = 4) 
(11,011 changes made to pgfamstd)

.         label values pgfamstd label_famst

.         
.         * Education
.         label define label_ed 1 "less than secondary" 2 "secondary" 3 "higher
> "  

.         recode pgisced97 (0/2 = 1) (3/4 = 2) (5/6 = 3)
(25,782 changes made to pgisced97)

.         label values pgisced97 label_ed

.         
.         * Labor force status
.         label define label_lfs 1 "employed" 2 "not employed" 3 "unemployed"

.         recode pglfs (min/0 = .)  (1/5 8/10 13 = 2) (6 = 3) (11 12 = 1)
(22,509 changes made to pglfs)

.         label values pglfs label_lfs

.         
.         * Age and age squared 
.         gen age = syear - gebjahr

.         gen age_sq = age^2

. 
.         * Household size
.         bys hid syear: egen hhsize = count(pid) 

.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 
.         
.         * We estimate an OLS regression with our constructed indicator of sup
> port for gender equality (sge) as the dependent variable on the number of dau
> ghters (ndaughters)
.         * as the treatment variable and further control variables (number of 
> children, marital status, sex, age, age squared, household income, household 
> size education
.         * labor force status and the survey year. A positive coefficient on t
> he number of daughters (with a p-value smaller than 0.1) empirically supports
>  H1.
. 
.         reg sge ndaughters i.nchildren i.pgfamstd i.sex age age_sq hghinc hhs
> ize i.pgisced97 i.pglfs i.syear  

